kyogre's families have been enjoying the  spring sunshine after a long winter that  saw momentous political events in this  central asian country  one often described as the most  democratic in the region  but that may be changing dalet  says the new president's plan to change  the constitution  could be a step backwards on the path  towards full democracy  kyrgyz people have a strong will towards  freedom we don't tolerate backward steps  away from democracy  there are more serious issues to be  solved in the country than the  constitutional referendum  such as unemployment but it's better not  to destroy what we have already achieved  so far  this is the day that the new president  sudhar japarov was freed from jail by  supporters during protests  after parliamentary elections were  annulled in october following  widespread allegations of vote buying he  was serving an  11 year prison term for kidnapping at  the time a crime he denies  because he was out of the country when  it happened  in the six months since sarah japaroff  was greeted by supporters on these steps  he has  changed the political landscape in  kyrgyzstan dramatically  even before he won the presidential  election in january he said  that the parliamentary system of  governments in kyrgyzstan had failed  he described the system as being  dysfunctional and corrupt  critics say japan's proposed changes to  the 2010 constitution  show he is trying to impose a more  authoritarian form of rule  one which weakens checks and balances  needed to prevent  abuses of power this uh  proposed text of the constitution is  going to change  the rules of the game at least at the  high level  uh toward serious centralization of  power  uh in the office of the president and to  such an extent  that basically no national level  institution will be able to do anything  without participation or sign off on it  by the president the names on the  plaques  are those killed during two of the three  revolutions kyrgyzstan has had  since independence following the breakup  of the soviet union 30 years ago  the fight for democratic rights is  something kyrgyz people are proud of  young members of the recently  established reform party are campaigning  in  municipal elections which are being held  on the same day as the referendum  the presidential rule implies personal  rule  there there's no room little room for  parties  because the the value of parties is  being  down it's being devalued parties are  being represented as something  western something that is imposed  back at the amusement park people enjoy  the warm weather  and rides there's no sign of the  political upheaval of recent months  or the new president's plans for  constitutional change  that many say would put the brakes on  decades  of democratic gains charles stratford al  jazeera  bishkek
 

